adduser: read the password from a pipe reliably

ShareOS hands the password to ksmbd.adduser on standard input instead of
with -p, because a command line can be read by every local user in the
process list. Without a terminal tcgetattr() fills in nothing, and the
erase character the prompt compares every byte with was whatever happened
to be on the stack: a password containing that byte lost characters.

Upstream: not sent (ShareOS specific use of the prompt)

--- a/adduser/user_admin.c
+++ b/adduser/user_admin.c
@@ -32,6 +32,12 @@
 
 	pr_info("Prompting for password\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * ShareOS: the password comes through a pipe (it must not be on the
+	 * command line). Without a terminal tcgetattr() fills in nothing, and
+	 * the erase character below would be whatever was on the stack.
+	 */
+	memset(&term, 0, sizeof(term));
 	tcgetattr(STDIN_FILENO, &term);
 	raw_term = term;
 
